SCORE 09.03 03/08/2009
-----------
* New algorithm version numbering: yy.nn.  yy = year, nn = sequence number of
  the release in that specific year.
* New treatment of bad GPS fixes: all fixes not marked as 3D are discarded.
  Too many exceptions occured due to taking into consideration non-3D fixes.
* Corrected bug in flight initialisation: in some cases, bad GPS fixes were
  not skipped for take-off detection. - OBSOLETE: see above
* New command line parameters:
  * -drel  : provide debug info for release detection algorithm
  * -deng  : provide debug info for engine detection algorithm
  * -dff   : provide debug info for free flight scoring algorithm
  * -ddt   : provide debug info for declared task scoring algorithm
  * -rm<hh:mm:ss> : (no space) Set release point manually at the fix at or
             just after the given time.
* Command line parameter -df modified: -df no longer selects debug, but only
  pipes the selected debug output to a file.


SCORE 0.6.4  16/02/2009
-----------
* Removed bug in declared task analysis, related to engine test run.
* Decreased default minimum ENL for engine detection from 500 to 400.
  Some glider / logger combinations generate less than 500 with engine running.
* Introduced "maximum number of Knicks" value to keep processing time
  of free-flight analysis within acceptable limits.
* Added new command line parameter -nknicksmxx, where xx is the maximum
  number of knicks.  Reducing the value will decrease processing time, but
  will also decrease the quality of the result.
  Current default value: 90.
  Minimum value: 50
  Maximum value: 999


SCORE 0.6.3  04/02/2009
-----------
* Improved removal of bad fixes at start of logger: if logger is started at
  another location as the one it was shut down, bad fixes of the previous
  location may be included.
  Find the start point / airfield with the first good fix.
* Declared Task: start line: width 2 km iso 1 km.
  Finish line width is kept at 1 km.
* Declared Task: start height is the lowest altituted between the start of
  the soaring block and the start line crossing.
* DT / FF: 1 extra turnpoint allowed if total distance ( not scoring distance )
  is equal to or greater than 200 km.
* No command line parameters:
  -nff :  do not perform free flight scoring.
  -ndt :  do not perform declared task scoring.
* New data in dat file, see file Readme. New fields are marked with an asterisk.


SCORE 0.6.2  01/10/2008
-----------
* Removed bug in free flight analysis: last leg not coorectly limited to 30 km.
* Improved Benelux detection: Benelux detected when logger start inside borders,
  even if not from airfield in database.
  Now required to include
  - "Files/Borders" directory.
  - in that dir: required files: Belgium.txt, Netherlands.txt, Luxemburg.txt
* New command line option -ea: automatic engine detection.
  Is the same as -e, but avoids error messages if no ENL in logger.


SCORE 0.6.1  18/05/2008
-----------
* Removed bug that appeared when logger file contains a lot of zero fixes.
  Included code to remove all zero fixes, regardles of any conditions.
  (start_flight.c)


SCORE 0.5.9  30/09/2007
-----------
* Updated list of certified loggers.
* Added command line option -gid:
  If used, the missing of the GID (Glider identification, or registration)
  header record will not generate a critical error, but only a warning
  message (nr. 710), both in the dat file and the txt file.


SCORE 0.5.8  01/09/2007
-----------
* Added check for time of flight declaration against time of start of flight.
  This was required to skipp declared task analysis in case of the
  use of the LX Navigation "quick task" feature.
  

SCORE 0.5.7  25/04/2007
-----------
* Removed bug in evaluation without test run
* Declared task evaluation algorithm re-written:
  Some situations caused exceptions which could not easily
  be treated with the previous algorithm.
  The algorithm now performs two passes:
  - one with beercan disactivated,
  - one with beercan activated.
* Added command line parameter -etmin
  This will specify the minimum ENL threshold for engine running detection.
  The ENL threshold used in the program will be the higher of the
  automatically detected threshold and the given threshold.
  The minimum ENL value should follow the parameter without blank in between.
  Example: -etmin300
* Added command line parameter -et
  This will specify the ENL threshold to use for engine running detection.
  The ENL value should follow the parameter without blank in between.
  Example: -et300
* The output text file includes the ENL threshold used for engine detection.
  The dat output file has not been modified.
 
    
